# The WorldInfo node provides general information about the simulated world,
# e.g., gravity, the physics time step, and the Newton solver tuning fields.
#
# ⚠ ODE WAS DELETED (commit bdc02139, 2026-08-08). Newton/MuJoCo is the only
# physics backend. Several fields below are RETIRED: they are still DECLARED
# because an undeclared field is a parse ERROR that takes a headless run's exit
# code to 1 and legacy worlds still carry them, but they are NOT READ and
# setting them does nothing. Each is marked "RETIRED" inline. If a field is not
# marked, it is live.

WorldInfo {
  w3dField MFString                      info                           []
  w3dField SFString                      title                          ""
  field    SFString                      window                         "<none>"
  field    SFFloat                       gravity                        9.81      # along the down axis, expressed in m/s^2
  field    SFFloat                       CFM                            0.00001   # RETIRED: ODE's global constraint-force-mixing parameter. NOT READ -- there is no ODE world to hand it to. Newton/MuJoCo's equivalent knobs are newtonContactKe / newtonContactKd / newtonImpratio below.
  field    SFFloat                       ERP                            0.2       # RETIRED: ODE's global error-reduction parameter. NOT READ. See CFM above.
  field    SFString                      physics                        "<none>"  # RETIRED: named an ODE physics plugin. Physics plugins were removed with the ODE backend; the field is still declared so legacy worlds load (an UNDECLARED field is an ERROR that takes a headless run's exit code to 1), but any value other than "<none>" is ignored with one parse-time warning.
  w3dField SFFloat                       basicTimeStep                  32        # expressed in ms
  field    SFFloat                       FPS                            60        # maximum frame per second
  field    SFInt32                       optimalThreadCount             1         # RETIRED: set ODE's physics thread count. NOT READ -- Newton's threading is the solver's own.
  field    SFFloat                       physicsDisableTime             1         # RETIRED: ODE auto-disabled a body idle for this long. NEWTON HAS NO BODY SLEEP, so nothing is ever disabled and this gates nothing. Consequence worth knowing: an empty /sim/contacts can no longer be excused as "the body is asleep", and GET /sim/contacts?wake=1 is a no-op that still costs two steps.
  field    SFFloat                       physicsDisableLinearThreshold  0.01      # RETIRED: ODE idle threshold [m/s]. NOT READ -- see physicsDisableTime.
  field    SFFloat                       physicsDisableAngularThreshold 0.01      # RETIRED: ODE idle threshold [rad/s]. NOT READ -- see physicsDisableTime.
  field    SFNode                        defaultDamping                 NULL      # RETIRED: default Damping node applied to every Solid. NOT READ -- the ODE damping call it fed is an empty function and damping is not plumbed to Newton.
  field    SFFloat                       inkEvaporation                 0         # make ground textures evaporate to white
  w3dField SFString{"ENU", "NUE", "EUN"} coordinateSystem               "ENU"     # X -> East, Y -> North, Z -> Up
  field    SFString{"local", "WGS84"}    gpsCoordinateSystem            "local"   # defines the world coordinate system (used by GPS node)
  field    SFVec3f                       gpsReference                   0 0 0     # defines the offset for each coordinate
  w3dField SFFloat                       lineScale                      0.1       # order of magnitude of the world in meters, used to display the optional rendering
  field    SFFloat                       dragForceScale                 30.0      # RETIRED IN EFFECT: scaled the force of the GUI's alt-drag on a Solid. The value still sizes the arrow and the newton figure the 3D view prints, but NO FORCE IS APPLIED -- OmSolid::addForceAtPosition is an empty function since the ODE deletion. Use the supervisor's add_force, which routes through applyExternalForceNewton and does work.
  field    SFFloat                       dragTorqueScale                5.0       # RETIRED IN EFFECT: same as dragForceScale -- OmSolid::addTorque is an empty function, so the GUI torque drag applies nothing.
  field    SFInt32                       randomSeed                     0         # defines the seed used internally by the random number generator
  field    MFNode                        contactProperties              []        # RETIRED: per-material-pair contact settings for ODE. NOT READ on the Newton path -- a world declaring coulombFriction 5 still runs at mu=1.0, which has cost real debugging time twice. Set friction with newtonGroundMu and contact stiffness/damping with newtonContactKe / newtonContactKd below. The engine warns once when a world declares this field. Note it is a per-material LIST and newtonGroundMu is ONE global value. ⚠ THIS LINE USED TO END "so a world using several materials has no exact migration" AND THAT IS NO LONGER TRUE: Solid.newtonFriction (added 2026-08-27) sets friction PER SOLID, so a gripper pad at mu=2 gripping a part resting on a table at mu=0.3 is now expressible. newtonGroundMu remains the world-level default that any Solid not declaring its own value inherits. ⚠ MuJoCo combines the two contacting geoms' friction by taking the elementwise MAX (measured, tests/physics/worlds/newton_per_solid_friction.omniworld), so raising ONE side of a contact is enough to raise that contact -- and lowering one side is NOT enough to lower it.
  field    SFString{"simple", "sap", "quadtree", "auto"} broadphase     "simple"  # RETIRED: selected ODE's broadphase collision structure. NOT READ -- the ODE context it configured was deleted with the backend. Newton/MuJoCo does its own broadphase and exposes no equivalent choice here. The value is accepted and ignored.
  field    SFString{"", "auto", "mujoco", "mujoco_warp", "mujoco+vbd", "vbd"} newtonSolver "" # Newton solver profile. ⚠ TWO VALUES ARE EXCEPTIONS TO THE "always SolverMuJoCo" sentence that follows, and they differ from each other in WHO OWNS THE RIGID BODIES. "vbd" = ONE newton SolverVBD owns EVERYTHING -- rigid bodies, joints AND particles -- with no SolverMuJoCo in the world at all. That is the only configuration in which a rigid body and a cloth particle are relaxed in the SAME solver sweep, which is what newton's own cloth-grasp example (examples/vbd/example_vbd_gripper_soft_grid.py) is built on and what a gripper closing on fabric needs: across a coupled proxy the cloth only ever sees a jaw whose pose was synced from the PREVIOUS rigid solve, so a pinch is resolved against a one-step-stale pad. THE PRICE IS LARGE AND MOSTLY INVISIBLE UNLESS YOU READ THE LOG. There is no mj_model on this path, so raycast-backed devices (DistanceSensor / Receiver / LightSensor / Radar / Camera-recognition occlusion), Connector / VacuumGripper welds, TouchSensor and the MuJoCo contact readback (getContactPoints, GET /sim/contacts, /sim/grips) are ALL gone; rigid contact becomes AVBD penalty / augmented-Lagrangian rather than MuJoCo's friction cone, so every MuJoCo tuning field below (newtonCone, newtonImpratio, newtonIterations, newtonLsIterations, newtonNoslipIterations, newtonNjmax, newtonNconmax) is unread; and newton ITSELF silently ignores five joint features OmniSim uses -- joint_armature, joint_friction, joint_effort_limit (Motor.maxTorque), joint_velocity_limit (Motor.maxVelocity) and joint_target_mode. That silence is real: newton's unsupported list (solver_vbd.py:119-135) is a class DOCSTRING, with no warning and no validation behind it, so OmniSim reads the finalized model at load and logs one "newtonSolver \"vbd\": IGNORED -- ..." line per feature the world actually uses. A DISTANCE joint is refused outright (SolverVBD does not implement it). Joint PD drives DO work: joint_target_ke / joint_target_kd are honoured on REVOLUTE, PRISMATIC and D6, so a motorised gantry or gripper is drivable -- but the gains are baked at solver construction, so changing them afterwards has no effect. OMNISIM_NEWTON_VBD_WORLD is the value-parsed override (=1 forces, =0 refuses). The other exception is "mujoco+vbd": it asks the runtime for a COUPLED pair -- SolverMuJoCo for the rigid articulation plus newton's SolverVBD for cloth particles, exchanged over one Model by SolverCoupledProxy -- and it is the value to reach for FIRST whenever a world has both a robot and fabric, because it is the only one that keeps MuJoCo's joint features and its friction cone. It is also the only one of the two that offers PER-BODY control of what the fabric can see: Solid.newtonClothCoupling narrows the proxy roster so the sheet collides with the gripper pads and not with all thirty links of the arm ("vbd" has no proxy and therefore no roster -- everything is in one solver by construction). A world with no Cloth node has no reason to set either value (the pair costs a second solver's setup for nothing); a world WITH a Cloth node and a purely rigid value here parses and loads, the Cloth registers no particles, and it renders frozen at its authored rest pose with one warning naming this field. Everything below still describes the rigid half, which "mujoco+vbd" leaves on the reference CPU mj_step path. The solver is SolverMuJoCo, always -- XPBD was REMOVED 2026-08-07 (zero of 725 worlds ever selected it; per newton's own docs it does not operate on articulations; it measured slower than CPU mujoco at every scale AND drove the shipped 10-Husky swarm 0.97 m where mujoco and ODE agree on 2.4 m; record: docs/developer/ode-retirement-campaign.md #0.5). What this field now selects is CPU vs GPU: "" / "auto" / "mujoco" = the reference CPU mj_step (deterministic; bitwise at 336 contacts); "mujoco_warp" = the same solver batched on the GPU -- pays off ONLY for parallel training (at nworld=1 it measured 9.06x slower than CPU), needs a CUDA GPU + an EVEN newtonSubsteps, and is not run-to-run reproducible. A world still declaring "xpbd" gets the parser's invalid-value warning and the default. Applies to every Solid: Newton is the only backend.
  field    SFInt32                        newtonClothSelfContact -1        # Whether cloth/soft PARTICLES collide with EACH OTHER (SolverVBD's particle_enable_self_contact, plus the radius/margin that go with it). -1 = unset, keeping the runtime default, which is ON -- newton's own default is OFF, and with it off a fold passes straight THROUGH itself with no error, no warning and no contact record, so draping (the normal case for cloth) is wrong out of the box. 0 = OFF, 1 = ON. ⚠ THE RIGHT VALUE DEPENDS ON WHAT THE WORLD IS FOR, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS 24x. Draping/folding needs it ON. GRASPING needs it OFF: measured on the patch world, a pinched fold's tracking error is -22.11 mm with self-contact ON and -0.92 mm with it OFF, because the fabric gathered between the pads pushes ITSELF out of the jaws. Every deformable-grasp world in this tree therefore wants 0. It also costs 0.99 ms/step (35%) on a 289-particle sheet, so OFF is cheaper as well -- but do not read that as "off is better": a folded sheet with it off is not simulating fabric, it is simulating a ghost. This was ENV-ONLY (OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT) until 2026-08-15, which meant a grasp world could not state its own physics: the file carried the scene and the shell carried the 24x. Forgetting the variable did not fail -- it slipped, silently, which is the worst failure shape available. The OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT env var still overrides (env > field > default) and is value-parsed, so =1 is the exact-revert hatch for a world declaring 0. Ignored by worlds with no particles: only newtonSolver "vbd" and "mujoco+vbd" build a SolverVBD, and the engine warns once if this field is set on a world that builds neither.
  field    SFInt32                        newtonSubsteps         1         # Newton sub-steps per physics tick. 1 = one solver step per tick (preserves the exact physics + determinism of every existing world + trained RL policy). N>1 splits the tick into N sub-steps of dt/N -- shrinks integrator truncation (measured: flips lane-1 T4 energy and T7 angmom in Newton's favour at N=4) but is NOT a free win: it degraded T2 sliding accuracy 12x in the same sweep, likely because MuJoCo's solref/solimp are not rescaled. The RL deploy stack runs N=8. On "mujoco_warp" the CUDA-graph replay needs an EVEN N. Folds the OMNISIM_NEWTON_SUBSTEPS launch knob into the world file (the env var still overrides). Applies to every Solid: Newton is the only backend.
  field    SFString{"", "pyramidal", "elliptic"} newtonCone      ""        # Newton/MuJoCo friction-cone type for SolverMuJoCo worlds. "" = MuJoCo stock (pyramidal). "elliptic" removes the inscribed-pyramid creep near the cone boundary (omnibench T2: 181 mm pseudo-slip below the static-friction transition at pyramidal, 0.6 mm at elliptic+impratio 10); pair it with newtonImpratio 10. The OMNISIM_NEWTON_CONE env var still overrides. NOT flipped as the process default: it changes contact physics for every Newton world and would break train==deploy bit-exactness of shipped champions (follow-up gated on champion re-verification). Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers (i.e. only newtonSolver "mujoco" / "mujoco_warp" read it).
  field    SFFloat                        newtonGroundMu         -1        # Newton contact friction (MuJoCo tangential mu) for the world's shapes. NEGATIVE = unset (the built-in 1.0); 0 is a LEGAL value meaning frictionless. (It used to be that 0 meant unset, so a frictionless world could not state itself in its own file -- only via the env var.) ⚠ WorldInfo.contactProperties coulombFriction is NOT read on the Newton path -- this is the field that sets friction there. Raise it for legged robots (a friction probe measured sphere feet sliding ~1 m while merely standing at mu=1.0 and planting inside 4 cm at mu=2.0); a two-finger friction pinch needed mu=3. Above ~6 the floor itself destabilises even at basicTimeStep 8. The OMNISIM_NEWTON_GROUND_MU env var still overrides. Applies to every Solid: Newton is the only backend.
  field    SFFloat                        newtonContactKe        0         # Newton contact stiffness (SolverMuJoCo maps a shape's ke to the geom's solref). 0 = unset (the built-in 2500). RAISE it (8000 measured) so gripper fingers stop AT an object's face instead of over-penetrating and squirting it out; LOWER it to soften contact for dense layers of small parts. The OMNISIM_NEWTON_CONTACT_KE env var still overrides. Applies to every Solid: Newton is the only backend.
  field    SFFloat                        newtonContactKd        0         # Newton contact damping (paired with newtonContactKe). 0 = unset (the built-in 100). The OMNISIM_NEWTON_CONTACT_KD env var still overrides. Applies to every Solid: Newton is the only backend.
  field    SFInt32                        newtonIterations       0         # Newton/MuJoCo solver iterations for SolverMuJoCo worlds. 0 = unset (the solver's own default). Raise for hard frictional contact that must HOLD rather than creep (150 measured for a two-finger pinch). The OMNISIM_NEWTON_ITERS env var still overrides. Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers (i.e. only newtonSolver "mujoco" / "mujoco_warp" read it).
  field    SFInt32                        newtonLsIterations     0         # Newton/MuJoCo line-search iterations for SolverMuJoCo worlds. 0 = unset (the solver's own default). Pairs with newtonIterations (50 measured alongside 150). The OMNISIM_NEWTON_LS_ITERS env var still overrides. Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers (i.e. only newtonSolver "mujoco" / "mujoco_warp" read it).
  field    SFFloat                        newtonImpratio         0         # Newton/MuJoCo impratio (frictional-to-normal constraint impedance ratio) for SolverMuJoCo worlds. 0 = unset (MuJoCo stock 1). Raise (e.g. 10) together with newtonCone "elliptic" to stiffen tangential contact for accurate static-friction hold. The OMNISIM_NEWTON_IMPRATIO env var still overrides. Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers (i.e. only newtonSolver "mujoco" / "mujoco_warp" read it).
  field    SFInt32                        newtonCondim           0         # Newton/MuJoCo contact dimensionality (mjModel.geom_condim) for SolverMuJoCo worlds. 0 = unset, leaving the model exactly as newton built it -- MEASURED to be condim 3 on EVERY geom of EVERY OmniSim world, so every existing world stays byte-identical. 1 = frictionless (mu is not consulted at all), 3 = sliding friction only, 4 = + torsional friction, 6 = + torsional and rolling. ⚠ At condim 3 the torsional and rolling coefficients newton writes into geom_friction[1] and [2] are NEVER READ, so a part pinched between two gripper pads is free to SPIN about the contact normal at zero cost, and a cylinder rolls forever on flat ground. 4 is what a two-finger friction grasp wants (NVIDIA's own newton gripper example sets condim=4 on the finger shapes); 6 is the rolling-resistance case OMNISIM_NEWTON_ROLL_MU covers. Applied by patching mj_model.geom_condim / mjw_model.geom_condim after solver construction -- it is GLOBAL, not per-shape: newton's per-shape `mujoco:condim` custom attribute needs SolverMuJoCo.register_custom_attributes(builder), which the engine does not call, and on the CPU mj_step path a post-finalize per-shape write would not reach mj_model anyway. Setting 4 or 6 also floors geom_friction[1] at 0.005 so the added dimension is not a no-op. The OMNISIM_NEWTON_CONDIM env var still overrides. Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers.
  field    SFInt32                        newtonNoslipIterations 0         # Newton/MuJoCo NOSLIP post-solve iterations (mjOption.noslip_iterations) for SolverMuJoCo worlds. 0 = unset (MuJoCo stock 0, i.e. the pass is OFF), so every existing world is byte-identical. It is MuJoCo's own Gauss-Seidel pass over the FRICTION constraints only, run after the main solve, whose entire purpose is removing the tangential DRIFT a soft friction constraint accumulates under a sustained load -- the failure that makes a two-finger pinch creep out of a grasp a millimetre at a time while its normal force is held at exactly the commanded value. MEASURED on ladder0 rung 8 (a 0.2 kg part pinched at 3 N/pad with mu=3, i.e. 9x the Coulomb bound, lifted 0.15 m and carried 0.45 m): at 0 the part creeps 56 mm down the pads during a 1.5 s lift and drops; from 1 upward it is carried, and the answer is INSENSITIVE to the count (identical from 3 up), which is what makes this a solver BUDGET rather than a tuning value -- 5 is past the knee with margin. Costs a friction-only Gauss-Seidel sweep per iteration per step. ⚠ CPU ONLY: mujoco_warp does NOT implement noslip (its Option struct has no such field and put_model RAISES on a non-zero value), so on newtonSolver "mujoco_warp" this field is ignored and the engine logs one WARNING rather than failing the build. Applied by writing mj_model.opt.noslip_iterations AFTER solver construction, because SolverMuJoCo has no kwarg for it and calls put_model on both paths. The OMNISIM_NEWTON_NOSLIP env var still overrides and is value-parsed, so =0 is the exact-revert hatch for a world that declares the field. Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers.
  field    SFInt32                        newtonNjmax            0    # Newton/MuJoCo constraint-row buffer cap (njmax) for SolverMuJoCo worlds. 0 = unset (keeps the engine default 256). A positive value raises the cap; -1 requests newton's own auto-estimate and is NOT RECOMMENDED (see the -1 warning below). mujoco_warp does NOT read the MJCF <size njmax> tag and its auto-estimate is far too small for multi-robot fleets, so the row budget must be declared. MEASURED on a flat-ground 10-Husky mujoco_warp world: a 4-wheel-drive Husky rests on 8 wheel-ground contacts and a pyramidal cone costs 2*(condim-1)=4 rows each, so 32 rows per robot -- ten of them peak at nefc=320 / ncon=80 and overflow the 256 default from tick 4 (rule of thumb: ~32*N rows for N wheeled robots, so 256 runs out at 9). An overflow SILENTLY TRUNCATES the constraint vector, degrading contact solving; mujoco_warp's own diagnostic is a per-tick "nefc overflow" wp.printf from inside the warp kernel, which floods where stdout is captured and is DISCARDED on Windows (omnisim-bin is a GUI-subsystem binary). The engine therefore samples the counters itself on the newtonSolver "mujoco_warp" path and emits ONE "[OmNewtonBackend] CONSTRAINT BUFFER OVERFLOW" WARNING into the engine log naming the observed peak nefc/ncon against the allocated caps; OMNISIM_NEWTON_CONSTRAINT_STATS=<every N steps> additionally traces every new peak into .build_tmp/newton_solver.log (=-1 turns the watch off). WARNING about -1: it does NOT mean "sized for this world". newton hands sizing to mujoco_warp's _default_njmax, which is computed from a single mj_forward at t=0 and floors at 64 for any world with no heightfield/flex/SDF geom -- i.e. every arena world here -- so on the 10-Husky fleet it resolves to ~64 against a 320 peak (~80% of rows dropped), STRICTLY WORSE than leaving this field at 0. It is kept only for scenes whose contact set is already complete at t=0; setting it logs a warning. Prefer 0, or an explicit positive value with headroom (512 for ten 4WD robots) -- sizing exactly to the measured peak is itself a trap (docs/benchmarks/determinism-scope.md sec.3: 8.81 m of divergence at njmax=320). Folds the OMNISIM_NEWTON_NJMAX launch knob into the world file so a fleet world is self-contained (the env var still overrides). Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers (i.e. only newtonSolver "mujoco" / "mujoco_warp" read it).
  field    SFInt32                        newtonNconmax          0         # Newton/MuJoCo contact buffer cap (nconmax) for SolverMuJoCo worlds. 0 = unset (keeps the engine default 256). A positive value raises the cap; -1 requests newton's own auto-estimate, which carries the SAME t=0-sizing trap as newtonNjmax -1 (read that field's note; setting it logs a warning). Overflow drops contacts and prints "broadphase overflow" / "narrowphase overflow" from the warp kernel, which nothing reads -- the engine's own overflow WARNING (see newtonNjmax) is what actually reaches the log. Raise it with newtonNjmax for multi-robot worlds (a contact costs 1 slot here and 1-4 constraint rows there). Folds the OMNISIM_NEWTON_NCONMAX launch knob into the world file (the env var still overrides). Ignored on non-MuJoCo Newton solvers (i.e. only newtonSolver "mujoco" / "mujoco_warp" read it).
  field    SFBool                         newtonStatics          FALSE   # ⚠ THE SCHEMA DEFAULT SHOWN HERE IS NOT THE ENGINE DEFAULT. Registers a world's top-level static colliders (have a boundingObject, no joint parent) as mass=0 Newton bodies so dynamic bodies collide against them. STATICS ARE ON BY DEFAULT since 2026-08-07 (OmSolid.cpp, flushPendingNewtonRegistrations: `bool staticsOn = true`), so leaving this field FALSE/absent still gets you solid floors, tables and walls. Precedence: this field TRUE pins statics ON and no hatch can take them away; otherwise OMNISIM_NEWTON_STATICS is value-parsed and "0"/"false"/"off"/"no" turns them OFF; unset = ON. Why the flip: with statics off a static collider collided with NOTHING -- a ball dropped onto a Box floor whose top is at z=0.55 fell through and settled at z=0.0996 on the implicit z=0 plane, a surface not in the file, with no warning and exit 0. Worlds whose floor sits at z=0 masked it. Pinned by tests/test_newton_static_floor_collides.py.
  field    SFBool                         newtonRobotColliders   FALSE     # Give robot-wrapper bodies (a URDFRobot chassis Solid) their OWN boundingObject as a Newton collider, so the robot BODY -- not just its wheels/feet -- collides with scene geometry. FALSE skips it: a chassis envelope box that engulfs the wheel space would pin the body and starve the wheels of load (P3.10i/j), so wheel-only collision is the safe default everywhere. TRUE folds the OMNISIM_NEWTON_WRAPPER_USES_OWN_SHAPE launch knob into the world file (the env var still forces it on) for chassis whose collider clears the wheels (e.g. the warehouse Husky). Applies to every Solid: Newton is the only backend.
  field    SFBool                         newtonCompoundColliders FALSE    # Register EVERY collider in a compound boundingObject (a Group of offset primitives on ONE rigid body -- e.g. a movable bin's floor + 4 walls, or an L-tube of two boxes) as its own Newton shape, instead of only the first child. FALSE = first-child-only, keeping every existing world's physics byte-for-byte unchanged. TRUE folds the OMNISIM_NEWTON_COMPOUND_COLLIDERS launch knob into the world file (the env var still forces it on) so a multi-box bin / part "just works" in the GUI / launcher / headless without an env var. Applies to every Solid: Newton is the only backend.
  field    SFString{"", "ode", "newton"}  defaultPhysicsBackend  ""        # World-level default physics backend. When set, supplies the backend for any Solid still on the "auto" default that has no explicit ancestor choice -- i.e. pin a whole world to "newton" without editing every Solid. "" = inert (each Solid resolves "auto" on its own, which also means Newton). ⚠ Pinning a WORLD to "ode" gives that world NO PHYSICS: ODE was deleted and the value is a retired selector, not a legacy engine. An explicit per-Solid / per-articulation-ancestor physicsBackend still wins. ⚠ The world-level field is spelled defaultPhysicsBackend; writing the per-Solid name `physicsBackend` inside WorldInfo is an ERROR line, which takes a headless run's exit code to 1 and reads as a crash.
  field    SFString{"", "wren", "wgpu"}   defaultRenderBackend   ""        # World-level default renderer (default-flip-plan.md §3.2). When set, supplies the backend for any Viewpoint/Camera whose renderBackend is empty or "auto". "" = inert. An explicit per-node renderBackend still wins. "wren" is RETIRED since 2026-08-23 (F1): it still parses, but each consuming node warns once and renders wgpu anyway -- it can no longer pin a world to the legacy renderer.

  # deprecated fields
  deprecatedField SFVec3f                northDirection                 0 1 0     # north direction is now defined by the coordinate system
  deprecatedField SFString               fast2d                         ""        # name of fast2d simulation
}
